  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(1,3)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(1,3)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,3.02).$",
  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\u2248f(a,b)+\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot \u27e8x-a,y-b\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(1,3)+\u2207f(1,3)\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =f(1,3)+\u27e8{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =4+\u27e82,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y-3\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,3.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02$ and $y=3.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,3.02) & \u22484+\u27e82,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e81.02-1,3.02-3\u27e9 \\\\  & =4+\u27e82,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80.02,0.02\u27e9 \\\\  & =4+0.04+0.02 \\\\  & =4.06\\end{aligned}\n$$",
